<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Good Friday Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1998</span> · Also known as Belfast Agreement · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-troubles">The Troubles</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Good Friday Agreement, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> Agreement, signed on 10 April 1998, was the settlement that largely ended the Troubles in Northern Ireland. Agreed by the British and Irish governments and most Northern Irish parties, it created a power-sharing Assembly and Executive and cross-border bodies, and confirmed that Northern Ireland would remain in the United Kingdom unless a majority voted otherwise. Ireland dropped its constitutional claim to the North, and referendums on both sides of the border approved it.</p></div>
